y", "plugin/lib/release_readiness.py", "tests/test_atomic_state_writes.py", "tests/test_change_cut.py", "tests/test_change_fragments.py", "tests/test_change_log_archive.py", "tests/test_consumer_digest.py", "tests/test_release_readiness.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-consumer-digest.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/release-plan-v3.8.0.md", ".prawduct/changes/consumer-digest.md", ".prawduct/project-state.yaml"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "blocking", "title": "A change-log refusal test was deleted with the coverage tests, and the commit's stated reason does not cover it", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: `test_a_mis_encoded_change_log_refuses_with_its_named_reason` was a method of the deleted `TestDigestCoverageIsAdvisory` class, so it went out with that class. Its subject was not the digest. It fed a non-UTF-8 `.prawduct/change-log.md` to `check_releasability` and asserted exit 1 plus `no-change-log:`, which is the refusing half of the encoding class, and that behaviour is unchanged (release_readiness.py prints `no-change-log: {read_error}` and returns 1). De33e054 justifies only 'coverage-advisory tests and tests of the deleted section-reading functions' as the CD13 requirement change. The Chunk 03 plan says 'nothing else is weakened'. What survives does not replace it at the CLI layer: `test_an_unreadable_log_is_a_read_error_even_beside_fragments` checks only that `pending_scopes().read_error` is set, and `test_no_log_and_no_fragment_directory_is_still_a_missing_input` covers a missing file, not a mis-encoded one. Restore the test without its `digest=` fixture argument. Show it red by narrowing `_UNREADABLE` (or the pending-set read's catch) to `OSError`.", "files": ["tests/test_release_readiness.py", "plugin/lib/release_readiness.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "Phase 0 ignores the release file, so the runbook's post-cut check on a pruned release tells the operator to delete the section the cut just wrote", "recommendation": "Scope: instance (one owner, `_digest_advisories`). Why it broke: Phase 0 copies CD7's `release-in-changelog` predicate but not where the cut checks it. `plan_cut` looks for `.prawduct/releases/<version>.md` first (the release's identity, Decision 2) and takes the re-run path. `_release_section_check` and `_headline_warnings` never look at that file. Here is how it is reached. The cut-and-publish runbook's step 4 runs `check-releasability --release vX.Y.Z` right after `cut-change-log --apply`. On a pruned release the withheld fragments are still pending, so the no-pending early return is skipped and the digest advisories run. Under prawduct's own `headline: required`, two warnings then fire, both false. One says `release-headline.md is absent ... refuses no-headline`, but the cut consumed that file. The other says `already has a section for vX.Y.Z ... Remove it, or move its text to .prawduct/release-headline.md`, and that section is the one the cut wrote. Following that remedy deletes the published section. A re-run of the cut then writes nothing, because the release file exists, so the section is not restored and the banner shows nothing. The old open-section advisories were correct at this step: the renamed top section carried the headline. Fix: when the release file for `release` exists, `_digest_advisories` should emit one line saying the release is already cut, naming the release file, and skip both checks, the way the cut does. Pin it with a `check_releasability` test that applies a cut with one withheld fragment and asserts that neither warning prints. Keep the pre-cut fixture as the control.", "files": ["plugin/lib/release_readiness.py", "tests/test_release_readiness.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "The design's CD2 one-reader pin was never built", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. 733-design.md's test plan maps CD2 to 'one reader, pinned by grep that `release_readiness` and `change_cut` read `consumer_digest` only through `read_declaration`'. No test in test_consumer_digest.py, test_change_cut.py or test_release_readiness.py does this. `test_the_deleted_section_machinery_stays_deleted` only scans for removed names, so a new second reader with a new name passes it. CD2 holds today: no plugin module other than consumer_digest.py reads the `consumer_digest` key. The likely regression is a reader that parses the key straight from a tag's tree, the way release_verification.py already reads `release_version_files` with `read_yaml_block`. That would reopen the two-homes split that Decision 1 closed, and nothing would catch it. Add the pin over plugin/lib/*.py and the hook: no `read_yaml_block(..., \"consumer_digest\")` and no `consumer_digest.DECLARATION_KEY` read outside consumer_digest.py. Show it red by adding a direct read to change_cut.py.", "files": ["tests/test_consumer_digest.py", "documentation/issues/733-design.md"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "priors: two accepted dispositions acknowledged, not re-raised", "recommendation": "Scope: none. `test-status` reports stale (evidence 2026-10-25T23:59:21Z predates the session). That falls under R-2, accepted as 'the declared suite runs and is recorded once at the boundary (Chunk 07)'. The later commits to change_cut.py and test_change_cut.py do not change that reason. The empty change fragment record-lint pair falls under R-5, accepted as Chunk 07's deliverable. `verify-coverage` is skipped (`coverage_required` is false).", "files": [".prawduct/changes/consumer-digest.md", "plugin/lib/change_cut.py", "tests/test_change_cut.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Phase 0 ignores the release file, so after a pruned cut it tells the operator to delete the section the cut just wrote", "recommendation": "Why it broke: the cut treats `.prawduct/releases/<version>.md` as the record that a release was cut (change_cut docstring; `_plan_rerun`). Phase 0's `_digest_advisories` never looks at it. It infers state from two other signals, whether the headline file exists and whether the changelog has a section. Runbook step 4 re-runs `check-releasability --release vX.Y.Z` right after `--apply`. On a pruned release the withheld fragments keep `pending` non-empty, so the digest advisories run and print two false warnings. One is `plugin/CHANGELOG.md already has a section for vX.Y.Z \u2026 Remove it, or move its text to .prawduct/release-headline.md`, which refers to the section the cut wrote itself. The other says `headline: required \u2026 release-headline.md is absent`, which the cut consumed. An operator who follows the first warning removes the published section while the release file stays. The cut's re-run then reads the release file as 'already cut' and never puts the section back, so the changelog and the release record diverge for good. Scope: instance (one function). Fix: in `_digest_advisories`, when `release` is known and `consumer_digest.release_file_rel(version)` exists, report that the release is already cut and skip both checks. Get the path from consumer_digest rather than spelling it again. Add the post-cut state to design Section 7, and add a test that runs Phase 0 after a pruned apply.", "files": ["plugin/lib/release_readiness.py", "documentation/issues/733-design.md", "tests/test_release_readiness.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "The heading-first headline warning only runs under `headline: required`, but the cut uses a headline file under either mode", "recommendation": "`_digest_advisories` calls `_headline_warnings` only when `decl.headline == \"required\"`. Yet `_release_writes` puts any present headline file into the section whatever the mode, and the emission comment in `_digest_advisories` says so itself ('the cut uses a present headline file either way'). Take a `headline: none` product whose headline file opens with `# \u2026`. The cut writes it under the release heading, the version-delta banner shows nothing, and Phase 0 never warns. That contradicts the `_headline_warnings` docstring's claim that it is 'the only place that one is caught'. Under `headline: none`, an unreadable headline file also prints `digest headline: none`, while the cut refuses `no-headline` on that same file. The records disagree too: design Section 7 lists the heading-first warning with no condition, and CD12 puts it under `headline: required`. Scope: instance. Fix: gate only the absent/empty-file warning on `required`. Run the heading-first and unreadable checks whenever the headline file exists. Make CD12 and Section 7 say the same thing, and add a `headline: none` heading-first test beside `test_headline_none_owes_no_headline`.", "files": ["plugin/lib/release_readiness.py", "documentation/issues/733-design.md", "documentation/issues/733-requirements.md"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "Phase 0 and the cut each re-derive two rules that consumer_digest is meant to own", "recommendation": "The consumer_digest module docstring says the cut and Phase 0 'are meant to ask it rather than keep \u2026 a heading rule of their own'. Two rules are still written out at their call sites. (1) The release-section scan, `for number, line in enumerate(text.lines, start=1): if consumer_digest.names_release(line, version)`, appears in both `release_readiness._release_section_check` and `change_cut._release_writes`, and the two remedies already read differently ('move its text to' versus 'move its first paragraph into \u2026 if it was the headline'). (2) `release_readiness._release_headline` works out the headline's first line by itself, although `consumer_digest.headline_lines` calls itself 'the one home of that rule'. The predicates stay in agreement because both call `names_release`, and the banner-agreement test pins the headline case, so nothing is wrong today. Scope: class, two members, both listed. Optional fix: add a `consumer_digest` function that returns the matching `(line_no, line)` pairs, and take the first line from `headline_lines`.", "files": ["plugin/lib/release_readiness.py", "plugin/lib/change_cut.py", "plugin/lib/consumer_digest.py"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "`consumer_digest.insert_section` is only called by tests", "recommendation": "No production code calls `insert_section`; it is a one-line wrapper that drops `place_section`'s line number. The cut calls `place_section`, and only `tests/test_consumer_digest.py` calls `insert_section`. That leaves the module's public surface with two ways to do one splice. Scope: instance. Delete it and point the test helper at `place_section(...)[0]`, or keep it knowingly. No other change is needed.", "files": ["plugin/lib/consumer_digest.py", "tests/test_consumer_digest.py"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "change_cut keeps calling private functions in sibling modules", "recommendation": "`change_cut` calls `release_readiness._find_release_plan` (now twice, one call added in `_plan_rerun`) and `change_log_archive._compose_bucket`. Before this bundle it also read `release_readiness._DIGEST_REL_PATH` and `_open_digest_section`. Those two are gone, which is an improvement. The rest predates this bundle and the tests would catch a rename, so this is a NOTE. Scope: instance. If these modules are touched again, make `find_release_plan` and `compose_bucket` public names. No edit is wanted for this chunk.", "files": ["plugin/lib/change_cut.py"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "The release runbook still describes the cut as it worked before the release-file model", "recommendation": "Since Chunk 04, `.prawduct/runbooks/cut-and-publish-a-plugin-release.md` step 3 is out of date. It says the cut 'inserts the consumer notes under step 2's headline'. Its undo note says to restore the archive and `.prawduct/changes/` and delete the added `**`<scope>`**:` paragraphs. Following that undo now leaves `.prawduct/releases/vX.Y.Z.md` behind and the consumed headline file gone, and the next cut then takes the 'already cut' re-run path. Plan Chunk 05 owns the rewrite and the file is outside this review's subject set, so this is a NOTE. It matters only if the bundle reaches a release path before Chunk 05 lands. In that case, correct the undo sentence now, following core.md's 'A chunk boundary defers a DELETION, never a CORRECTION'. Scope: instance.", "files": [".prawduct/runbooks/cut-and-publish-a-plugin-release.md"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "An unreadable project-state.yaml reads as 'undeclared', so the cut writes no release section and says the repo declares none; the recorded reason is false", "recommendation": "Scope: instance (consumer_digest.read_declaration's `except (OSError, ValueError): return None, None`). Why it broke: one except clause lumps 'absent' together with 'present but unreadable'. CD2 and design Section 1 justify that by saying the two 'cannot be told apart from one that never declared'. They can. FileNotFoundError is absent. IsADirectoryError, PermissionError and UnicodeDecodeError mean a file exists that could not be read. The consequence lands on the writer. plan_cut gets (None, None), archives the fragments and removes them. It prints 'this repo declares no consumer_digest:', which is false, and writes no release file, so no re-run guard exists either. Phase 0 prints nothing. That release's notes never reach the declared changelog. This is fail-open on the authority path, against the plan's own 'Authority fails closed' and against the module's own line that 'an operator who wrote the key meant something'. The sibling reader in release_readiness (blocker liveness) fails CLOSED on a project-state that will not load. Learnings: hook-surface.md, 'Pick each fallback's direction from the invariant it protects: an unknown bucket blocks'. Fix: return (None, None) only on FileNotFoundError. Return (None, '<path> cannot be read: <exc>') otherwise, so the cut refuses `digest-declaration-invalid` and Phase 0 warns. Then amend CD2's sentence and design Section 1's 'cannot be told apart' clause to match. The existing test_an_absent_or_unreadable_state_file_reads_as_undeclared (a directory standing in for the state file) is the assertion to renegotiate in the open.", "files": ["plugin/lib/consumer_digest.py", "documentation/issues/733-requirements.md", "documentation/issues/733-design.md", "tests/test_consumer_digest.py"], "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "Records Pass", "severity": "warning", "title": "Records Pass: CDOC3 (the release runbook) is owned by no chunk, and Chunks 03/04 already falsified runbook instructions no later chunk's deliverables cover", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: the plan's chunks name CD1\u2013CD15 and CDOC1\u2013CDOC2, but no chunk names CDOC3. Chunk 01's acceptance check covers the design, not the plan. Bar cleared: 'misleads into action'. An agent building Chunks 05\u201306 from this plan follows Chunk 05's deliverables ('procedure text for bumps, reopen and the release body') and Chunk 06's list (templates, key registry, docstrings). Neither covers the runbook sections this bundle has already made false. In .prawduct/runbooks/cut-and-publish-a-plugin-release.md: ~145\u2013151 describe `digest headline: '\u2026' in ## vX.Y.Z-dev.N` and `digest coverage: N of M`; ~259 describes `NOTE: digest coverage not checked`; ~336 describes `consumer notes: N added under '## vX.Y.Z-dev\u2026'`; ~360\u2013368 describe `no-headline` as an open-section condition and the deleted `wrong-section` refusal; ~385\u2013393 tell the operator to write the headline as the first line of the open `## vX.Y.Z-dev` section. The table also lacks the new codes (digest-missing, digest-unreadable, release-in-changelog, releases-ignored, marker-missing, digest-declaration-invalid, release-already-cut). Chunk 05's sweep query (`grep -rn \"\\-dev\"`) misses the coverage-NOTE line and the refusal-code rows. So the v3.8.0 operator can meet a runbook that asks them to fill a section the cut now ignores. Learnings: authoring.md, 'A chunk boundary defers a DELETION, never a CORRECTION \u2014 if your chunk falsifies a sentence a later chunk owns, fix the falsehood now'. Fix: name CDOC3 in a chunk and list these runbook sections in its deliverables, or correct them now. Records Pass covered set: architecture.md, build-plan-consumer-digest.md, release-plan-v3.8.0.md, changes/consumer-digest.md, project-state.yaml. Only this item cleared a bar.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-consumer-digest.md", ".prawduct/runbooks/cut-and-publish-a-plugin-release.md"], "fid": "R-12"}, {"goal": "Learnings Cross-Check", "severity": "warning", "title": "Learnings Cross-Check: the owner ruling that lets the plugin write a product's changelog is witnessed only by files this bundle wrote", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The new 'Ruling: 2026-10-25 (consumer-digest, #733), owner-ruled in session (\"1 yes\")' beneath architecture.md's 'writes nothing into a governed repo' clause widens a Direction norm. Its authority line cites documentation/issues/733-requirements.md Decision 7, which the same agent wrote in this same bundle (2fff1a69). #733's issue body records the owner choosing the changie model, but it does not record this write permission. The two earlier rulings in that block cite the issues where the owner's ruling sits (#942, #928). The clause's wording is correctly left alone. The gap is only where the confirmation is witnessed. Learnings: core.md, 'A governance change cannot supply its own authority: when an agent amends a binding norm mid-build, land the owner's confirmation somewhere the amendment isn't \u2014 a change that is its own only witness reads as laundering'. Consequence: every product that declares consumer_digest: has its own file written under a permission whose only record is the builder's account of a conversation. A later reviewer or PR reviewer cannot tell it apart from a self-granted widening. Fix: record the owner's 'yes' outside this bundle (on #733, quoting the question asked and the answer), and point the ruling's authority clause at that record.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", "documentation/issues/733-requirements.md"], "fid": "R-13"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "scope-trace: consumer_digest.insert_section has no production caller", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Since 2476a251, change_cut calls place_section directly. insert_section ('place_section without the line') is now called only by tests/test_consumer_digest.py:210, and design Section 3 still lists it as API. The capability itself (byte-preserving insert) is consumed end to end, so this is a dead alias rather than an unreached capability. Fix: delete it, and point the test and the design's signature at place_section. Or keep it, knowing it is a test convenience in the public API.", "files": ["plugin/lib/consumer_digest.py", "documentation/issues/733-design.md"], "fid": "R-14"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Decision 8's recorded why points at a requirement id that does not exist (CDW8)", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Decision 8 ends 'Legacy products are pointed at fragments instead (CDW8)', but no CDW-prefixed requirement exists. The legacy pointer is CD10's second sentence, so this is probably a leftover from an earlier draft's numbering. A reader checking where the retired coverage check's subject went finds nothing. Fix: replace the citation with CD10, or delete the parenthetical. Prose NOTE; nothing reads it mechanically.", "files": ["documentation/issues/733-requirements.md"], "fid": "R-15"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions and inferences awaiting the owner, for the owner's read", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Alignment pass: 'Proceed, citing #733', with the three owner decisions recorded (Decisions 1, 2/3, 7). Mid-build decisions in the plan and what settled each: (1) write_all_or_none widened to newline=\"\" rather than given a bytes sibling ('every fact has one home'), and render returns str; (2) two added declaration refusals, a heading that does not lead with the version and an entry without {note} ('authority fails closed'); (3) ordered_notes moved into consumer_digest, with render_consumer_section deleted in Chunk 04; (4) Chunk 04 delegate choices: trailing-whitespace-insensitive headline match, a non-UTF-8 headline as no-headline, release-already-cut without a plan, a legacy line only for ships-classified scopes, insert_line as the heading's line, and the section-flow golden renegotiated as a release-in-changelog refusal; (5) the default {date} uses local time rather than UTC, pinned by a red-shown test; (6) delegate B's worktree was based on main, caught and verified; (7) #1072 mitigation (explicit --chunks). Chunk 03 records no mid-build decision; its code matches design Section 7 as written. Still marked 'Inferred, owner can veto', so the owner should confirm them before the boundary PR: Decision 8 (the coverage check retires), the by-type default map (feature\u2192Added, fix\u2192Fixed, else Changed), and the default heading `## {version} - {date}`. The plan's decision entries are dated 2026-10-26 while the commits are 2026-10-25 local. That is inert, and no edit is wanted.", "fid": "R-16"}, {"goal": "Learnings Cross-Check", "severity": "note", "title": "Learnings Cross-Check ran over core.md, authoring.md, hook-surface.md, release.md and tests.md", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Read list from `prawduct-hook learnings-files --for-diff`. Two rules fired as escalations and are cited in the findings above: core.md 'A governance change cannot supply its own authority' and authoring.md 'A chunk boundary defers a DELETION, never a CORRECTION'. hook-surface.md 'Pick each fallback's direction' backs the read_declaration finding. The change conforms to several rules it could have broken. 'Preserve line endings in any writer editing a file it didn't create': write_all_or_none and ChangelogText use newline=\"\" both ways. 'A mechanical is-it-finished test keyed on a REUSED identifier archives live work': holds_entry keys on Block.key, not the scope name. 'A parser shared by a READER and a WRITER inverts its safety': the writer refuses a heading it cannot recognise. 'Editing a norm's STATEMENT in the same commit as the code it would bless': the clause is left as ratified. No learnings rule was added or changed in this interval, and no implicit supersession was found.", "fid": "R-17"}, {"goal": "Backlog Reconciliation", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog reconciliation: no open item is resolved by this bundle", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Cache read OK: brookstalley/prawduct, coverage ~24 min old, no sync_error. `affecting` over the changed lib files and plugin/CHANGELOG.md returned #733, #762 and #928. #733 is this work and stays open: CD14, CD15 and CDOC1\u20133 are Chunks 05\u201307. Do not mark it shipped until the 'When to mark shipped' rule applies, after v3.8.0. #762 (a wholesale .prawduct/ gitignore defeats the contract) is not resolved. The new releases-ignored refusal is a sibling guard, and _contract_diff is untouched. #928 is unrelated. #1069 resolves as a redirect to #733 (folded, dropped), so the citations are not dangling. C-B1/C-B2: no new item filed in the interval. C-B3: the release-tooling area's only open item touched is #733 itself. C-B4: the cited ids #733, #1069, #1072, #942 and #928 all resolve.", "fid": "R-18"}], "counts": {"blocking": 1, "warning": 7, "note": 10}, "